---
extras: ["nocull"]
title: Placing the exit
---
Put the exit near a point you choose, or let a Dijkstra map find the tile that is farthest from the start [[t:244-250 s:26 s:27]].

## The rule
{% table label="Where the exit goes, by setting" rowheads=true %}
* Setting
* The exit goes on
---
* `right` (c18)
* the open tile nearest the middle of the right edge [[c18:92-109]]
---
* `farthest` (c19)
* the tile with the largest walking distance from the start, read from a Dijkstra map [[c19:92-101]]
{% /table %}

Then the route from the start to the exit is found [[c18:111 c19:103]].

- **Demo:** the route is the one its library’s A* search returns [[c18:111 c19:103]].
- **Ours:** always a shortest path; the demo’s [isn’t always](#exit-faithfulness) [[own]].

- **Talk says:** `farthest` is the least accessible part of the map, for when you really dislike your player [[t:249-250]].
- **Talk says:** with a start in the middle, that part is a really bad idea for an exit, and a great way to hide something [[t:249-256]].

## Step-through
@@steps limit=4

## Controls
@@control at
`right`: the open tile nearest the middle of the right edge [[c18:92-109]]. `farthest`: the tile with the largest walking distance from the start [[c19:92-101]].

## Measured traits
@@measured

- **Left edge to right:** a route of {{c18.path.median}} steps at the median [[run:c18.path.median]].
- **Center to farthest:** {{c19.path.median}} [[run:c19.path.median]].
  - **Why shorter:** a start in the middle has less map on any side of it [[own]].

## Failures
@@fails

- **Without the cull:** in a recipe of our own, the exit lands in a cave the start can’t reach on {{nocull.fails.exit-unreachable}} of 200 seeds [[run:nocull.fails.exit-unreachable]]. [Seed 0]({{recipe:nocull}}) is one.

## Combinations
@@combines

## Faithfulness
@@checked

- **The route:** the talk’s is longer than the shortest walk between its own start and exit on {{c18.astar.longer}} of 200 seeds in `c18`, and on {{c19.astar.longer}} in `c19` [[run:c18.astar.longer c19.astar.longer]].
  - **Why the path trait still agrees:** it counts the shortest walk on both sides [[own]].
